## Why Fetch Data at Build Time?

In Next.js, pages are **pre-rendered** into HTML on the server. To include dynamic data in this initial HTML for fast loads and SEO, the data must be fetched **before** the page component is rendered. This approach, known as Static Site Generation (SSG), is different from traditional client-side fetching in React (using a `useEffect` hook), which fetches data in the browser after the initial page load.

## The `getStaticProps` Function

To fetch data at build time for a specific page, you export a special `async` function called **`getStaticProps`** directly from that page file (e.g., `pages/ninjas/index.js`).

> [!IMPORTANT]
> `getStaticProps` only runs on the server at **build time**. The code inside it will never be executed in the user's browser.

**Key Characteristics:**
- It must be `export`ed from a page.
- It's an `async` function, allowing you to use `await` for asynchronous operations like API calls with **`fetch`**.

## Passing Data to Components

The `getStaticProps` function must return an object. This object must contain a **`props`** key, whose value is another object containing the data you wish to pass to your page component.

```javascript
export async function getStaticProps() {
  // Fetch data from an external API
  const res = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users');
  const data = await res.json();

  // Pass data to the page via props
  return {
    props: { ninjas: data },
  }
}
```

> [!TIP]
> The page component then receives this data automatically via its props. You can destructure it for easy access: `const Ninjas = ({ ninjas }) => { ... };`

## Rendering the Static Data

Once the data is available in your component as a prop (e.g., `ninjas`), you can use standard JavaScript methods like `.map()` within your JSX to iterate over the data and render a list of elements. Remember to always provide a unique `key` prop to the root element for each item in the list, just as in regular React.

```jsx
const Ninjas = ({ ninjas }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>All Ninjas</h1>
      {ninjas.map(ninja => (
        <div key={ninja.id}>
          <a>
            <h3>{ninja.name}</h3>
          </a>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```
